WEBVTT


1
00:00:01.600 --> 00:00:04.240
A full-fashion decrease is a technique

2
00:00:04.440 --> 00:00:09.560
where we manipulate stitches, so
the decreases form a decorative element.

3
00:00:09.760 --> 00:00:15.680
In this video, we'll explore single,fine-tuning
double and triple decreases.

4
00:00:15.880 --> 00:00:22.080
Please watch to the end where we'll give
a tip for fine-tuning this technique.

5
00:00:22.280 --> 00:00:25.040
to work a single full-fashioned decrease,

6
00:00:25.240 --> 00:00:31.440
we transfer one stitch, emptying a needle,
putting two stitches on one needle,

7
00:00:31.640 --> 00:00:36.400
and then we transfer the edge stitches
in to fill the empty needle,

8
00:00:36.600 --> 00:00:42.000
reducing the number of stitches
in work by one, a single decrease.

9
00:00:42.200 --> 00:00:46.520
The extra steps create
this decorative element.

10
00:00:46.720 --> 00:00:50.920
The decreases can be worked,
any number of stitches from the edge.

11
00:00:51.120 --> 00:00:56.920
Here, we've decreased one stitch,
three stitches in from the edge.

12
00:00:57.120 --> 00:00:59.520
Multiple stitches can be decreased

13
00:00:59.720 --> 00:01:04.960
the same way with the same
decorative full-fashioned effect.

14
00:01:05.160 --> 00:01:09.760
For a double decrease transfer
and double up 2 stitches.

15
00:01:09.960 --> 00:01:13.120
There are now two empty needles.

16
00:01:13.320 --> 00:01:16.200
Then transfer the edge stitches in to fill

17
00:01:16.400 --> 00:01:21.960
those empty needles
and two stitches are decreased.

18
00:01:33.240 --> 00:01:37.880
A triple decrease is worked the same way.

19
00:01:38.080 --> 00:01:41.080
But transferring 3 stitches is really

20
00:01:41.280 --> 00:01:47.120
stretching it, so it's best to work
a triple decrease in steps.

21
00:01:47.320 --> 00:01:50.760
First, take off three of the stitches
on a three-pronged tool

22
00:01:50.960 --> 00:01:56.240
and rotate the tool to get those
stitches and the tool out of the way.

23
00:01:56.440 --> 00:01:59.040
Next, using whatever method works best

24
00:01:59.240 --> 00:02:04.880
for you, transfer those edge stitches in.
Filling those empty needles.

25
00:02:05.080 --> 00:02:10.040
In this example, I'm leaving
a 4 stitch edge. Again,

26
00:02:10.240 --> 00:02:14.120
transferring those stitches across
3 needles is way too much.

27
00:02:14.320 --> 00:02:17.040
So I'll just transfer a few at a time

28
00:02:17.240 --> 00:02:22.240
preventing any stretching
or drop stitches.

29
00:02:22.760 --> 00:02:24.920
Once all of the empty needles are filled

30
00:02:25.120 --> 00:02:31.200
in, I'll rehang those held
stitches doubling up.

31
00:02:31.400 --> 00:02:35.440
And I've decreased 3 stitches.

32
00:02:36.320 --> 00:02:39.600
If you're lucky enough to have
a multi-pronged transfer tool,

33
00:02:39.800 --> 00:02:44.920
it can make transferring those
stitches go more quickly.

34
00:02:59.560 --> 00:03:00.880
Here are the results,

35
00:03:01.080 --> 00:03:06.720
we've decreased multiple stitches
and created a nice decorative feature.

36
00:03:07.600 --> 00:03:13.040
This technique is especially helpful when
knitting a European shoulder sweater.

37
00:03:13.480 --> 00:03:17.040
This design moves the shoulders shaping
to the back of the sweater,

38
00:03:17.240 --> 00:03:21.040
and the shaping becomes
an integral part of the design1

39
00:03:21.240 --> 00:03:25.520
As you start manipulating the stitches
for double and triple decreases.

40
00:03:25.720 --> 00:03:27.560
You'll notice that we're stretching

41
00:03:27.760 --> 00:03:31.560
and distorting those stitches.
Right off the machine

42
00:03:31.760 --> 00:03:34.320
You can see my wonky stitches.

43
00:03:34.520 --> 00:03:37.800
plus the edge of the sample is a bit tight.

44
00:03:38.000 --> 00:03:42.000
This is just because of how
we moved the stitches around.

45
00:03:42.200 --> 00:03:49.040
I found that if I manually massage those
stitches a bit and worked any slack over

46
00:03:49.240 --> 00:03:53.080
to the edge stitch,
it not only cleaned up those stretched

47
00:03:53.280 --> 00:03:58.400
wonky stitches, but it
loosened up the edge as well.

48
00:04:02.280 --> 00:04:05.600
Another tip is to alternate your decreases.

49
00:04:05.800 --> 00:04:11.480
if your pattern is telling you to decrease
three stitches four times and two stitches

50
00:04:11.680 --> 00:04:18.080
three times, alternate the double
and triple decreases for a better result.

51
00:04:18.280 --> 00:04:19.880
Please give this technique a try

52
00:04:20.080 --> 00:04:25.120
on a swatch and try this little tip
for perfection and you'll be ready when

53
00:04:25.320 --> 00:04:28.600
you need to do double and triple
decreases in your knitting.

